---
title: Any-bean soup
description: Lesson 3.4. The module together, with ingredients you choose.
servings: 4
time: 50 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, wet-heat]
lesson: "3.4"
technique: building a soup
status: draft
---

> Technique: building a soup. Sweat the base, add things in order of
> how long they take, simmer without boiling, and season at the end.
> The ingredients are slots: pick what you have for each one and keep
> the amounts.

Heat @olive oil{2%tbsp} in a #large pot{} over medium-low heat. Add @aromatic vegetables{300%g}(onion, carrot, celery or leek, finely chopped) and a pinch of salt. Cook for ~{10%minutes}, until soft and not coloured.

> Why: this is sweating, from lesson 3.1. While the vegetables are
> wet the pan cannot go above 100 °C and they soften without browning.

Add @hard vegetables{200%g}(potato, squash or swede, in 1 cm dice) and @stock{1%litre}(vegetable or chicken). Bring to a boil, then turn down to a simmer and cook for ~{10%minutes}.

> Why in stages: dense vegetables can take much more cooking than
> tender ones, so they go in first.
-- source: "7 Tips for Successful Soup Making", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-10-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/6226-7-tips-for-successful-soup-making

Add @tinned beans{2%tins}(400 g each; cannellini, butter beans or chickpeas, drained and rinsed) and simmer for ~{10%minutes} more. For a thicker soup, mash a ladleful of the beans against the side of the pot.

> Why simmer: at a boil the liquid is at 100 °C and churning, which
> breaks vegetables apart. A simmer, at about 82 to 88 °C with small
> bubbles now and then, cooks them whole.
-- source: "Cooking Class: Boil versus Simmer", Cook's Illustrated, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/cooksillustrated/how_tos/10660-cooking-class-boil-versus-simmer ; "7 Tips for Successful Soup Making", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-10-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/6226-7-tips-for-successful-soup-making

Stir in @leafy greens{100%g}(spinach, kale or chard, chopped) and cook for ~{3%minutes}, until wilted.

Take the pot off the heat. Taste the soup. Add @salt{1/2%tsp} a pinch at a time, tasting between pinches. Then stir in @lemon juice{1%tbsp} and taste again.

> Science: salt and acid do different jobs. Salt makes the flavours
> that are already there stronger; acid balances them, and makes the
> mouth water. Add the salt first, until the soup tastes full, and the
> acid last, until it tastes bright. Look up: taste interactions,
> salivation.
-- source: Eden Faithfull, "Put Down the Salt and Season Your Food with Vinegar", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-11-30, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/4073-put-down-the-salt-and-season-your-food-with-vinegar (Paul Adams of Cook's Illustrated, quoted; no test is described)

> Next time, without the recipe: something from each slot, added in
> order from longest-cooking to shortest, simmered and never boiled,
> then salted and sharpened at the end.
